Subject: [PATCH] Linux hosts: the C library and paths behave as on Windows; GS thread ended before the window; rumble

The runtime's C library stubs called host functions whose results differ between MSVC and glibc:
- strcpy, strncpy, strcat: the game shifts strings in place (strcpy(buf, buf + n), its file system's directory
  tree at 0x18DAD0 / 0x18DD90). Overlapping copies are undefined for the host functions: glibc's strcpy garbled
  the paths, so files in TFA.ZIP were not found and the first level stopped with "Generic fatal error"
  (geometry.graph). They copy with memmove now.
- strcmp, strncmp: the sign only (-1/0/1, as MSVC's); glibc returns the byte difference.
- rand, srand: MSVC's generator on every host, per thread as there (glibc's is another one).
- fseek / ftell: the offset is the guest's 32-bit long, sign-extended (an LP64 long kept -4 as 4294967292); ftell
  past 2 GB fails as UCRT's does.
- printf family: %p as UCRT prints it (uppercase, pointer-sized), NaN spelt as UCRT spells it (nan(ind), nan(snan)),
  %a with UCRT's default precision, long double as a double (MSVC's long double is 64-bit).
- the IOP's strtol / strtoul: 32-bit long results, overflow and the bare "0x" as UCRT handles them.
- localtime / gmtime: refused outside UCRT's range (1970 to 3001).
- case folding (tolower, toupper, isalpha in paths, device names, ROM names): ASCII only. GLFW sets LC_CTYPE from
  the environment on X11; in some locales (Turkish I) the host functions folded otherwise.
- ps2_settings.cpp: the non-Windows branch built a std::string from (char *, const char *): it did not compile.

Paths: Windows finds a file whatever its case; Linux now does too for the game's files, the memory card and the host
device (the exact name first, a case-blind match in each folder only when it is missing; two names differing only by
case are refused rather than one picked).

At exit the GS thread is ended before CloseWindow, and gives up its GPU context first: the context (a hidden
window sharing the main one's GL) was still current on the GS thread while glfwTerminate destroyed it, and Mesa's
Zink crashed in that teardown (an abort, or a jump into a driver already unloaded).

ps2_xinput_rumble: on Linux the game's vibration goes to the first gamepad with evdev force feedback (FF_RUMBLE):
Steam's virtual pad under Steam, else an Xbox pad's own driver. Checked through Steam's virtual pad (SteamOS).

Stick response, Linux: while a game menu is shown (the port says so: setMenuShown) the sticks reach the game as they
are, without the movement's anti-deadzone, which turned a small resting drift (a Steam Deck stick) into a held
direction and the menu cycled on its own. In play the inner dead zone is 12% (PS2X_STICK_DEADZONE; Windows keeps 5%):
the anti-deadzone made a stick resting just past 5% walk the robot and turn the camera. Gameplay keeps the response.
